# Line-ending policy.
#
# Every text file is stored in the repository with LF. Pinning it here means the
# result no longer depends on each contributor's local `core.autocrlf` setting.

# Detect text files and normalize them to LF on commit. Working-tree endings
# follow the platform unless overridden below.
* text=auto

# Binary-compatibility-validator dumps. `./gradlew apiDump` writes these with LF
# on every platform, so on a Windows checkout (where `core.autocrlf=true` gives
# them CRLF) the next apiDump rewrites them as LF and Git reports every dump as
# modified even though the ABI is unchanged. Pin the working-tree form to LF so
# the file on disk matches what the tool produces.
*.api text eol=lf

# POSIX shell script: must stay LF in the working tree, including on Windows, so
# it runs under Git Bash and WSL. Windows shells use gradlew.bat instead.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Windows batch script. This is a vendored file regenerated by `gradle wrapper`,
# which emits CRLF, and it is the one blob in the repository already stored with
# CRLF. `-text` disables conversion in both directions so it stays byte-identical
# to what Gradle ships instead of being renormalized to LF.
*.bat -text

# Binary assets: never apply text or line-ending conversion.
*.jar  binary
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
